Running the experiments
=======================

Here is a guideline to how to run four workloads: ycsb_redis, wrk, nginx_download, iperf3

1. Installations:
    - At the server VM: redis-server, nginx and iperf3 on your server (you can use apt install for them)
    - At the client (current guest VM): YCSB (https://github.com/brianfrankcooper/YCSB/tree/master), iperf3, axel
        ~ For YCSB, clone it as a sibling of linux-cs538-guest directory

2. Specify the server address or IP in server.txt

3. ycsb_redis workload:
    - Check if redis-server at the server VM is running (systemctl status redis-server)
    - If not running, start it (sudo systemctl start redis-server)
    - On the client guest VM, go to linux-cs538-guest directory
    - Run "sudo bash" followed by "bash workloads/ycsb_redis"
    - It may take some 30-45 minutes (for a server VM in the same cluster) or more (for a remote server VM)

4. wrk workload:
    - Check if nginx at the server VM is running (systemctl status nginx)
    - If not running, start it (sudo systemctl start nginx) - in case of any conflict check if other http servers e.g., apache are running which you may need to stop before staring nginx
    - On the client guest VM, go to linux-cs538-guest directory
    - Run "sudo bash" followed by "bash workloads/wrk"
    - It may take some 5-10 minutes (for a server VM in the same cluster) or more (for a remote server VM)

5. nginx_download:
    - Make sure nginx at the server VM is running
    - On the client guest VM, go to linux-cs538-guest directory
    - Run "sudo bash" followed by "bash workloads/nginx_download"
    - It may take some 5-10 minutes (for a server VM in the same cluster) or more (for a remote server VM)

6. iperf3:
    - Run the iperf3 server at the server VM with iperf3 -s
    - On the client guest VM, go to linux-cs538-guest directory
    - Run "sudo bash" followed by "bash workloads/iperf3"
    - It may take some 5 minutes to run

7. The output files are be generated in a sibling folder of linux-cs538-guest named output (file name will contain the name of the workload, kernel version and timestamp). These files contain the detailed logging of events including compilation, runtime messages and desired metrics.
    - For ycsb_redis, look up "run redis" and scroll down until you find the overall throughput
    - For wrk, nginx_download and iperf3 the output logs are shorter and more intuitive



